Logical analysis of mathematical statements:
    
1. truth table and negation:
    If A is a statement, the negation "¬" is called a logical operator

    The statement "¬A" is called a logical expressions
    
    The truth table shows all the outputs of the statement and logical expression
        Ex. __________
           |__A_|_¬A__|
           |__T_|__F__|
           |__F_|__T__|

    note: in the truth table, we usually consider statement A as a variable which may has T or F value. Based on this, sometimes we may
        call A as statement variable.

2. conjunction and disjunction
    conjunction - means connects together -> and 
    disjunction - opposite conjunction -> or

    Compound statement:
        compound statement is a statement composed of several individual statements. each of which is called compound statement

    Conjunction:
        The truth value for A and B, written sybomatically by A ∧ B, is defined by the truth table:
            ___________________
           |__A_|__B__|__A∧B___|
           |__T_|__T__|___T____|
           |__F_|__F__|___F____|
           |__T_|__F__|___F____|
           |__F_|__T__|___F____|

        Ex. (1+2=3) ∧ (3+1=4) -> true

    Disjunction:
        The truth value for A and B, written sybomatically by A ∨ B, is defined by the truth table:
            ___________________
           |__A_|__B__|__A∨B___|
           |__T_|__T__|___T____|
           |__F_|__F__|___F____|
           |__T_|__F__|___T____|
           |__F_|__T__|___T____|

        Ex. (1+2 != 3) or (1+2 = 3) -> true

    Some laws about conjunction and disjunction:
        De morgan's law:
            ¬(A∧B)≡¬(A)∨¬(B)
            ¬(A∨B)≡¬(A)∧¬(B)

        Commutative law:
            A∨B ≡ B∨A
            A∧B ≡ B∧A

        associatative law:
            A∧(B∧C) ≡ (A∧B)∧C
            A∨(B∨C) ≡ (A∨B)∨C

        distributive law:
            A ∧ (B ∨ C) ≡(A ∧ B) ∨ (A ∧ C)
            A ∨ (B ∧ C) ≡(A ∨ B) ∧ (A ∨ C)

3.implication
    the truth value for "A implies B", written symbolically as A ⇒ B is defined by the truth table:
            ___________________
           |__A_|__B__|__A⇒B__|
           |__T_|__T__|___T____|
           |__F_|__F__|___T____|
           |__T_|__F__|___F____|
           |__F_|__T__|___T____|
    component A is referred to as the hypothesis, B is referred to as the conclusion

    p ⇒ q 
    p -> hypothesis: something you know
    q -> conclusion: something you want to know

    the implication B ⇒ A is called the converse of A ⇒ B
         B ⇒ A !≡ A ⇒ B

    the implication ¬B ⇒ ¬A is called the contrapositive of A ⇒ B
        A ⇒ B ≡ ¬B ⇒ ¬A

    note: since from the truth table, we discover that if A is F, the result of A ⇒ B is always true.
        That is, if we want to determine the truth of the logical expression, we only consider the two 
        situations when A is T.

    note2: For statement variables A, B and C, we have:
        ((A ∨ B) ⇒ C) ≡ ((A ⇒ C) ∧ (B ⇒ C))

    note3: For statement variables A and Bm we have:
        (A ⇒ B) ≡ ((¬A) ∨ B) ,  ¬(A ⇒ B) ≡ (A ∧ (¬B))

4. if and not if
    the truth value for "A if and only if B", written symbolically as A B, is defined by the truth table:
            ___________________
           |__A_|__B__|__A⇔B__|
           |__T_|__T__|___T____|
           |__F_|__F__|___T____|
           |__T_|__F__|___F____|
           |__F_|__T__|___F____|

    note: For statement variable A and B, we have:
        (A ⇔ B) ≡ ((A ⇒ B) ∧ (B ⇒ A))

    note2: we also have the logical equivalence for univerally quantified statements
        (∀ x ∈ X, P (x) ⇔ Q(x)) ≡ (∀ x ∈ X, P (x) ⇒ Q(x)) ∧ (∀ x ∈ X, Q(x) ⇒ P (x))


    